<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#thrall">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thrall</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as þræll</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A thrall was a slave or serf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, the lowest of the three orders of Norse society, below the freeman (karl) and the nobleman (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a>). Thralls were property, bought and sold; people became thralls by birth, debt, punishment or capture in war. Most captives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids were sold abroad, many to Muslim merchants for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>; those kept tended animals, fished and farmed. Thralls could be freed or buy their freedom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was phased out from the 12th century until the last Swedish county abolished it in 1335.</p></div>
